\subsection{\code{TradeableInstrument$place_order()}}{
  Places one order in this instrument through UBI.

UBI chooses the broker itself, so no broker is named here. The values are sent exactly as given, without rounding the price to the tick size or checking the quantity against the lot size, because UBI and the broker behind it hold those rules.

UBI couples the price fields to the order type and answers HTTP 400 when they do not agree: a \code{limit} or \code{sl} order needs a price, an \code{sl} or \code{sl-m} order needs a trigger price, and a \code{market} or \code{sl-m} order must carry no price at all. A \code{price_reference} stands in for the price and a \code{quantity_reference} for the quantity.

An outcome of \code{accepted} means the broker took the order, not that the order survived. The exchange can still refuse it afterwards, which is what happens to an ordinary order sent while the market is closed, so the order's real fate is read from \code{orders} rather than from this answer. Neither this class nor UBI checks the market's hours, so use \code{after_market} to queue an order for the next session.

Every order goes through UBI's order engine, which is the only way UBI places orders. A plain \code{limit} order with a price of its own, \code{day} validity, no \code{synthetic} object and \code{after_market} \code{FALSE} is not sent to a broker straight away: the engine holds it as a \code{virtual_limit} order and sends it only once the other side of the book reaches its price, answering HTTP 202 with an outcome of \code{armed}, a \code{parent_id} and no \code{order_id}. Such a held order is changed with \code{modify_order(parent_id = ...)} and cancelled with \code{cancel_parent()}, and it never appears in \code{orders} until it has been sent. Pass \code{synthetic = list(type = "simple")} to send a limit order at once, which matters for an instrument that has no live quote, because the engine would hold its order for the whole day without ever sending it.

A plain \code{market} order with no \code{synthetic} object and \code{after_market} \code{FALSE} is not sent as a market order either. The engine runs it as a \code{marketable_limit} order: a \code{limit} two ticks past the other side's best price, moved after that price until it fills, with whatever is left cancelled 30 seconds after it was placed. Such an order is refused with HTTP 409, and nothing is sent, when nobody is on the other side of the book, no live quote has arrived or the quote is marked stale. Pass \code{synthetic = list(type = "simple")} to send a real market order, which an instrument with no live quote needs.

The examples below, in order:
\itemize{
\item Have UBI build a limit buy for one Vodafone Idea share without sending it, and print the request it would send.
\item Place a plain limit buy 3 per cent below the market, which the order engine holds until a seller reaches the price, and cancel it.
\item Describe the price rather than state it, here the third best bid, and see the price UBI works out in a dry run.
}
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{TradeableInstrument$place_order(
  transaction_type,
  order_type,
  quantity,
  product,
  price = NULL,
  trigger_price = NULL,
  validity = NULL,
  disclosed_quantity = NULL,
  after_market = FALSE,
  tag = NULL,
  dry_run = FALSE,
  price_reference = NULL,
  quantity_reference = NULL,
  synthetic = NULL
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{transaction_type}}{The character side of the order, \code{"buy"} or \code{"sell"}. UBI overrides it for a quantity reference that reduces or closes a position.}
      \item{\code{order_type}}{The character kind of order, \code{"market"}, \code{"limit"}, \code{"sl"} or \code{"sl-m"}.}
      \item{\code{quantity}}{The integer quantity in underlying units, not lots, or \code{NULL} when a quantity reference supplies it.}
      \item{\code{product}}{The character product, \code{"cnc"} for delivery, \code{"mis"} for intraday or \code{"nrml"} for carry forward.}
      \item{\code{price}}{The numeric limit price in rupees, or \code{NULL} for an order type that takes no price or when a price reference supplies it.}
      \item{\code{trigger_price}}{The numeric trigger price in rupees, or \code{NULL} for an order type that takes no trigger.}
      \item{\code{validity}}{The character validity, \code{"day"} or \code{"ioc"}, or \code{NULL} to let UBI use \code{"day"}.}
      \item{\code{disclosed_quantity}}{The integer quantity to show on the exchange, or \code{NULL} to disclose the whole order.}
      \item{\code{after_market}}{A logical that is \code{TRUE} to send the order as an after-market order.}
      \item{\code{tag}}{A character label of up to twenty letters and digits for the order, or \code{NULL}.}
      \item{\code{dry_run}}{A logical that is \code{TRUE} to have UBI build the broker's request and return it without sending it.}
      \item{\code{price_reference}}{A named list that describes the price instead of stating it, such as \code{list(kind = "offer_level", level = 2)}, which UBI resolves from the live quote and rounds to the tick, or \code{NULL}. Its \code{kind} is \code{absolute}, \code{bid_level}, \code{offer_level}, \code{mid}, \code{vwap}, \code{last} or \code{marketable}, and it may carry \code{price}, \code{level}, \code{buffer_percent}, \code{offset_percent} and \code{offset_ticks}.}
      \item{\code{quantity_reference}}{A named list that describes the quantity instead of stating it, such as \code{list(kind = "liquidate_position", product = "intraday")}, which UBI resolves from the positions, or \code{NULL}. Its \code{kind} is \code{add_to_position}, \code{reduce_position} or \code{liquidate_position}, and its optional \code{product} is spelled the positions' way.}
      \item{\code{synthetic}}{A named list that makes the order one of UBI's synthetic order types, such as \code{list(type = "bracket", stop_price = 990, stop_limit_price = 988, target_price = 1010)}, or \code{NULL} for a plain order. The synthetic order classes, such as \code{BracketOrder}, build it.}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Details}{
    Errors: signals \code{BadRequestError} when a field is invalid, the price fields do not fit the order type, or a synthetic order's own fields are wrong; \code{LossLockoutError} when the day's loss is past UBI's daily loss limit; \code{NotFoundError} when no broker has a mapping for this instrument; \code{ConflictError} when a quantity reference asked to reduce or close a position that is not held, a market order sent as a marketable limit found nobody on the other side of the book or no fresh quote, a reduce-only order would not reduce the position, or the engine read the order too late or had already started it before a restart; \code{OrderRejectedError} when the broker refused the order, and the detail holds its answer; \code{RateLimitError} when the broker's daily order cap has no room for this order; \code{ServiceUnavailableError} when no broker could take the order, the order engine is not running, or a price reference could not be resolved; \code{OrderOutcomeUnknownError} when the order was sent but its outcome is unknown, so read the order book, or \code{Account$intent()} with the detail's \code{intent_id}, before sending it again; and another \code{UnifiedBrokerInterfaceError} subclass for any other failure reported by, or on the way to, UBI.
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A named list with \code{broker}, \code{instrument_id}, \code{order_id}, \code{outcome}, \code{status_message}, \code{broker_response}, \code{skipped}, \code{timing_ms} and \code{intent_id}, and \code{parent_id} for an order the engine recorded, or, for a dry run, a named list with \code{dry_run} and the \code{request} UBI would have sent. The \code{order_id} is \code{NULL} unless the outcome is \code{accepted}. A held limit order, and a synthetic order that is waiting for a price or a time, answers with an outcome of \code{armed} and a \code{broker} and \code{order_id} of \code{NULL}. The types that send several orders at once add a \code{legs} list, one entry per order with its plan \code{path}, \code{instrument_id}, \code{outcome}, \code{order_id} and \code{status_message}, and their \code{outcome} is \code{partial} when some of those orders were accepted and some were not.
  }
  \subsection{Examples}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r example copy">}}
    \preformatted{idea <- Equity$new(exchange = "nse", symbol = "IDEA")

price <- round(idea$last_price * 0.97, 2)
answer <- idea$place_order(
  transaction_type = "buy",
  order_type = "limit",
  quantity = 1,
  product = "cnc",
  price = price,
  dry_run = TRUE
)
print(answer)

idea <- Equity$new(exchange = "nse", symbol = "IDEA")

price <- round(idea$last_price * 0.97, 2)
answer <- idea$place_order(
  transaction_type = "buy",
  order_type = "limit",
  quantity = 1,
  product = "cnc",
  price = price
)
tryCatch(
  {
    cat(
      answer[["outcome"]],
      answer[["parent_id"]],
      answer[["order_id"]],
      "\n"
    )
  },
  finally = {
    print(idea$cancel_parent(answer[["parent_id"]])[["state"]])
  }
)

idea <- Equity$new(exchange = "nse", symbol = "IDEA")

answer <- idea$place_order(
  transaction_type = "buy",
  order_type = "limit",
  quantity = 1,
  product = "mis",
  price_reference = list(
    kind = "bid_level",
    level = 3
  ),
  dry_run = TRUE
)
print(answer)
}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
}

