\subsection{\code{TradeableInstrument$buy_at_limit_price()}}{
  Buys at a price of your choosing, or better.

A limit buy never pays more than the price given. It waits in the market until someone sells at that price or lower, and it may never fill at all.

UBI's order engine holds a \code{day} limit order that is not an after-market order rather than resting it at a broker, and sends it only once the other side of the book reaches the price, so an order that never fills costs no order messages. Until then \code{place_order} answers with an outcome of \code{armed} and a \code{parent_id} rather than an \code{order_id}, the order is not in \code{orders} but in \code{parents}, and it is changed with \code{modify_order(parent_id = ...)} and cancelled with \code{cancel_parent}.

The examples below, in order:
\itemize{
\item Bid for one Vodafone Idea share 3 per cent below the market, which the order engine holds until a seller reaches the price, and cancel it.
\item Send the bid to the broker at once instead of letting the engine hold it, wait for it to rest in the order book, and cancel it.
}
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{TradeableInstrument$buy_at_limit_price(
  price,
  quantity,
  product,
  validity = NULL,
  after_market = FALSE,
  tag = NULL,
  hold = TRUE
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{price}}{The numeric limit price in rupees.}
      \item{\code{quantity}}{The integer quantity in underlying units, not lots.}
      \item{\code{product}}{The character product, \code{cnc} for delivery, \code{mis} for intraday or \code{nrml} for carry forward.}
      \item{\code{validity}}{The character validity, \code{day} or \code{ioc}, or \code{NULL} to let UBI use \code{day}.}
      \item{\code{after_market}}{A logical that is \code{TRUE} to send the order as an after-market order.}
      \item{\code{tag}}{A character of up to twenty letters and digits to label the order with, or \code{NULL}.}
      \item{\code{hold}}{A logical that is \code{TRUE} to let UBI's order engine hold a \code{day} order until the other side of the book reaches the price, and \code{FALSE} to send it to a broker at once. An after-market order is always sent at once, whatever this says. Pass \code{FALSE} for an instrument with no live quote, whose order the engine would otherwise hold all day without sending.}
