\subsection{\code{TradeableInstrument$buy_at_marketable_price()}}{
  Buys now with a limit order priced at the best offer, the price it takes to fill immediately.

This is what a market order has become in India: brokers convert an API market order into a limit order with price protection, and some refuse market orders outright. A marketable limit states the cap itself, so the order fills at once up to that price and never beyond it. UBI reads the best offer when it sends the order, and \code{buffer_percent} moves the cap that far above it to reach deeper into the book. Pair it with \code{validity = "ioc"} to cancel whatever cannot fill at once.

The examples below, in order:
\itemize{
\item Buy one Vodafone Idea share at once with a limit at the best offer as an immediate-or-cancel intraday order, then sell it straight back.
\item Reach half a per cent past the best offer, which still fills at the best price available but tolerates the book moving, and sell back what filled.
}
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{TradeableInstrument$buy_at_marketable_price(
  quantity,
  product,
  validity = NULL,
  after_market = FALSE,
  tag = NULL,
  buffer_percent = NULL
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
