\subsection{\code{TradeableInstrument$sell_at_marketable_price()}}{
  Sells now with a limit order priced at the best bid, the price it takes to fill immediately.

This is what a market order has become in India: brokers convert an API market order into a limit order with price protection, and some refuse market orders outright. A marketable limit states the cap itself, so the order fills at once up to that price and never beyond it. UBI reads the best bid when it sends the order, and \code{buffer_percent} moves the cap that far below it to reach deeper into the book. Pair it with \code{validity = "ioc"} to cancel whatever cannot fill at once.

The examples below, in order:
\itemize{
\item Sell one Vodafone Idea share short at once with a limit at the best bid as an immediate-or-cancel intraday order, then buy it straight back.
\item Reach half a per cent below the best bid, which still fills at the best price available but tolerates the book moving, and buy back what sold.
}
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{TradeableInstrument$sell_at_marketable_price(
  quantity,
  product,
  validity = NULL,
  after_market = FALSE,
  tag = NULL,
  buffer_percent = NULL
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{quantity}}{The integer quantity in underlying units, not lots.}
      \item{\code{product}}{The character product, \code{cnc} for delivery, \code{mis} for intraday or \code{nrml} for carry forward.}
      \item{\code{validity}}{The character validity, \code{day} or \code{ioc}, or \code{NULL} to let UBI use \code{day}.}
      \item{\code{after_market}}{A logical that is \code{TRUE} to send the order as an after-market order.}
      \item{\code{tag}}{A character of up to twenty letters and digits to label the order with, or \code{NULL}.}
      \item{\code{buffer_percent}}{The numeric percentage to move the cap past the best bid, such as 0.5, or \code{NULL} for no buffer. A cap too far from the market is refused by the exchange's price protection.}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Details}{
    Errors: signals \code{ServiceUnavailableError} when UBI could not work the price out, because there is no live quote, the order book is not that deep or no tick size is agreed, which is what the book looks like outside market hours; \code{BadRequestError} when a field is invalid; \code{OrderRejectedError} when the broker refused the order; \code{UnifiedBrokerInterfaceError} when any other failure reported by, or on the way to, UBI.
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    The named list \code{place_order} returns, holding \code{broker}, \code{order_id}, \code{outcome} and the rest.
  }
