\subsection{\code{TradeableInstrument$add_to_position()}}{
  Makes an existing position bigger, or opens a new one.

The direction follows the position you already hold: a long position is added to by buying and a short one by selling, so \code{transaction_type} is needed only when you hold nothing yet. Holding nothing also means there is no position to read a product from, so \code{product} is needed then too.

Only positions held under \code{cnc}, \code{mis} and \code{nrml} are visible here. UBI also reports positions under \code{margin_trading}, \code{cover} and \code{bracket}, which come from order kinds it cannot send, and those are ignored as though they were not there.

The examples below, in order:
\itemize{
\item Add one Vodafone Idea share to the intraday position with a limit a per cent above the market, so it fills at once even at a broker that refuses market orders, then add a second without naming a side, and sell both back until the position is where it started.
\item Show that naming a side against the position held is refused, because a sell would reduce a long position rather than add to it.
}
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{TradeableInstrument$add_to_position(
  quantity,
  product = NULL,
  transaction_type = NULL,
  price = NULL,
  validity = NULL,
  after_market = FALSE,
  tag = NULL
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{quantity}}{The integer quantity to add, in underlying units and always positive, whichever way the position points.}
      \item{\code{product}}{The character product of the position to add to, \code{"cnc"}, \code{"mis"} or \code{"nrml"}, or \code{NULL} when only one position is held.}
      \item{\code{transaction_type}}{The character direction to open in, \code{"buy"} or \code{"sell"}, used only when no position is held yet.}
      \item{\code{price}}{The numeric limit price in rupees, or \code{NULL} to send a market order.}
      \item{\code{validity}}{The character validity, \code{"day"} or \code{"ioc"}, or \code{NULL} to let UBI use \code{"day"}.}
      \item{\code{after_market}}{A logical that is \code{TRUE} to send the order as an after-market order.}
      \item{\code{tag}}{A character label of up to twenty letters and digits for the order, or \code{NULL}.}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Details}{
    Errors: signals \code{PositionError} when several positions are held and none was named, the direction given contradicts the position held, or nothing is held and no direction and product were given; and a \code{UnifiedBrokerInterfaceError} subclass for any failure reported by, or on the way to, UBI.
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    The named list \code{place_order()} returns, holding \code{broker}, \code{order_id}, \code{outcome} and the rest.
  }
